---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import F from "../data/found.json";
// People read out of a register whom the GEDCOM does not have. They have been
// recorded in sources/found.psv since September and published nowhere, which
// is what work-list row 22 was about. Two of them are on the direct line.
const direct = F.rows.filter((r) => r.ahn);
const rest = F.rows.filter((r) => !r.ahn);
const places = Object.entries(F.places).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base title="People Found Beside the Tree"
  eyebrow="Read out of a register, and not in the GEDCOM"
  dek="Eighteen people this archive has read off a page and the family tree has never heard of — two of them direct ancestors. They are kept beside the tree rather than put into it, and the reason is a rule, not an oversight.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every other source here keys on a slug from the family tree, so a person could
    only be recorded if <strong>MyHeritage already knew about them</strong>. On 15 September 2026
    ahnentafel 54's baptism named his father and his mother and <strong>there was nowhere to put
    them</strong>. This is the nowhere.</p>
    <p><strong>{F.count} people, {F.onDirectLine} of them on the direct line.</strong> They are
    deliberately given <strong>no slugs</strong> and are <strong>not merged</strong>: the tree is an
    export from MyHeritage and this archive does not edit it. What it does instead is put its own
    reading beside it, with the citation, so the two can be compared rather than blended.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>This list has been wrong, and the way it was wrong is worth
    knowing.</strong> «Not in the tree» was once decided by searching the published people — and a
    person can be in the tree with <strong>no page of their own</strong>, as a parent, a spouse, a
    sibling or a child named on somebody else's record. Four rows came out of this list on 15
    September for exactly that reason, and <strong>one of them was Hedviga's husband</strong>.
    <a href={u("/stones/")}>The stone that says so →</a></div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{direct.length} on the direct line</div>
    <h2>Ancestors the tree does not have</h2>
    <p>These are not collaterals. They are people the descent runs through, named in a register,
    absent from the family tree.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Who</th><th style="width:16%">Where and when</th>
        <th>What the page says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{direct.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{r.name}</strong>
            <span class="dek" style="display:block">ahnentafel {r.ahn} — {r.relation}</span></td>
          <td class="dek">{r.place}<span style="display:block" class="mono">{r.date}</span>
            <span style="display:block">{r.event}</span></td>
          <td style="font-size:.92em">{r.citation}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">{rest.length} more</div>
    <h2>Brothers, sisters, children and wives</h2>
    <p>Collaterals are not clutter. A sibling read off a page fixes a household to a house number,
    and a house number is what tells seven men called Michael apart —
    <a href={u("/oto/")}>which is the whole of question 4</a>.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Who</th><th style="width:16%">Where and when</th>
        <th>What the page says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rest.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{r.name}</strong>
            <span class="dek" style="display:block">{r.relation}</span></td>
          <td class="dek">{r.place}<span style="display:block" class="mono">{r.date}</span>
            <span style="display:block">{r.event}</span></td>
          <td style="font-size:.92em">{r.citation}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Where they were read</div>
    <h2>Nine of the eighteen are Karlobag</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      {places.map(([p, n]) => (<tr><th>{p}</th><td>{n}</td></tr>))}
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">That is not what Karlobag holds. It is what has been
    <strong>read</strong> — the parish whose films this archive has opened most often, which is a
    statement about the reader and not about the record.
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>What has been read →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The check</div>
    <h2>A claim that somebody is not in the tree now has to survive a gate</h2>
    <p><code>tools/foundcheck.py</code> tests every row here against <strong>every name in the
    tree</strong> — the published people and every parent, spouse, sibling and child reference —
    two ways: <strong>name and year within one</strong>, and <strong>name and both parents
    agreeing</strong>. A resemblance somebody has examined and dismissed is written down with the
    reason. Anything else <strong>fails the build</strong>.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Three resemblances stand and are settled in writing. The most
    interesting is <strong>Matilda Papić</strong>, ✽1895 †1921, third name on
    <a href={u("/stones/")}>her parents' cross at Senj</a>. The tree has no Matilda — but it has
    <strong>two Papić daughters born 1895</strong>, Franka and Sulka, and
    <strong>neither has a death recorded</strong>. She is probably one of them. That is an open
    question with a date attached, which is worth more than a guess.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
